build the shelf's ground on a thread on the way back from a game

Leaving a game froze the screen for about half a second. Traced on the
handheld, 2026-10-31, v0.4.3 with Config/trace-latency.txt, ejecting
Advance Wars:

    slot: faces: compose waited 66.2 ms for a cart face
    slot: long present: advance 100.3 step 0.0 render 426.5 (list 13.1 end 5.8 overlay 0.0)

One frame of 526 ms. The 100 ms in advance is the eject's own save write,
kept on the UI thread on purpose so the shelf never shows the cart out
before its data is down. Of the 426 ms render, 13 is drawing and 66 the
face wait (next-eject-faces-unwaited); the rest is upload_ground. The
shelf's ground and its blur are freed while a game plays (0018's memory, for a GBA cart to run), and the
first frame back decoded both 720x480 PNGs and resized them to the panel, in
line.

Now that work runs on a thread from the first frame after the game. The
two textures are created at once, transparent, so the app holds the same
ids it always did, and a frame waits for the art only when its own draw
list uses one of them. On this panel an eject first powers the screen off
over the game for POWER_OFF_S, drawing a black rect and nothing of the
shelf, so the build has that time free; if it is not done when the shelf
is drawn the frame waits as it did before. No frame shows less than it did.

The resize itself, cover_rgba, worked out each output pixel's source window
from floats per pixel; the windows depend only on the column and the row,
so they are worked out once each. Same float expressions, so the same
windows and the same bytes: a test keeps the old per-pixel version as the
reference and compares both real backgrounds at both panel sizes plus
synthetic up and down scales, byte for byte. 7.2 ms to 6.0 on the host for
the ground, decode included; labels go through it too.

The frontend's render tests are macOS-only and were not run here. slot's
and slot-ui's tests fail in the same 25 places with and without this patch.
Not yet run on the handheld.

Applies on top of 0037, upstream 91499a9.

